// The style sheet basically relies on macro name and value pairs.  In the style sheet file
// (named menu_style.mns for the game to find it) you define a macro name and follow it on
// the same line with a value consisting of text.  When .mnu and .mnx files are processed,
// those macro names -- in the tagged form "%NAME%" (without quotes) -- will be searched for.
// If found, the entire tagged macro name will be replaced with the value string associated
// with that named macro.  Macro name comparison is case-insensitive.  Obviously, any tagged
// macro names encountered that aren't in the style sheet file will cause a failure.  It is
// also an error when duplicate macro names are encountered in the style sheet file.
// The style sheet syntax is very simple.  Define a macro name consisting of any characters
// except whitespace or the following six: " % < > # \ / ".  Follow it on the same line with
// at least one whitespace character (space or tab) with the value.  The first non-whitespace
// character will be considered the beginning of the value string.  The value string can contain
// any character except '\' (if you need the backslash in your string, escape it with another
// backslash, i.e. "\\").  The end of the value string will be the last non-whitespace character
// on the line.
// You can continue a value string onto another line with a single backslash character.  In
// that case, the string will contain all characters leading up to the backslash (including
// whitespace), and all characters on the next line beginning with the first non-whitespace
// character.  You can also follow a macro name with a backslash, in which case the value string
// will begin with the first non-whitespace character on next line (this is the only instance
// in which a macro name can exist on a line by itself).
// Comments consist of two forward slashes (like this line) and can be placed anywhere in the
// style sheet file.  When encountered, the forward slashes and everything else following them to
// the end of the line will be ignored.  If you place a comment at the end of a value string, the
// end of the value string will be the last non-whitespace character before the comment (or the
// last character before a continuation backslash charater).
// Another feature is available that can be useful in development -- simple conditional blocks.
// These take the form "#if 1", "#if 0", "#else" and "#endif".  The ONLY valid arguments to the
// "#if " directive are '0' or '1'.  Any macro definitions (or partial definitions) following
// "#if 0" will be ignored up to the next associated "#else" or "#endif".  Obviously, the "#else"
// directive reverses the condition of the preceeding "#if" directive.  The conditional blocks can
// be nested, i.e. complete "#if-#else-#endif" blocks can be contained within other blocks.  Be
// careful, though -- incomplete conditional blocks will result in an error.  In is not recommended
// that style sheet files in consumer builds contain conditional blocks!
// At this point in time, any errors parsing the style sheet can, unfortunately, only be evaluated
// in a debug build of the game -- there is no error reporting mechanism hooked into this.  When
// an error does occur, please review your style sheet file for syntactical mistakes before
// consulting a programmer.

DEF_FONTNAME				Gunpl22b.fnt
